  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I didn't open the dashboard in Kibana or check that the rule has run.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web server access logs. The main fields are:\n- `@timestamp`\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`\n- `response`, plus `response.keyword`\n- `extension`, plus `extension.keyword`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `request`, `url` and `referer`\n- `agent`\n- `tags`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-782e5438 Web logs overview\" (id `ca94cd15-6d13-4a60-b516-4b250560b648`) has six panels and a default time range of the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- 5xx responses\n- Total bytes\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Requests by file extension\n\nAll six panels use ES|QL queries against `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-782e5438 5xx spike\" (id `41d65a17-4d0e-43f8-bf4b-cab28d4adb60`) is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **Query:** it counts documents in the last 5 minutes where `response.keyword` starts with \"5\".\n- **Condition:** it fires when that count is above 10.\n- **Actions:** it has none configured, so a firing only creates an alert in Kibana and sends no notification.",
